hostname r2
!
ip routing
!
! ============================================================
! r2 — Area Border Router (ABR)
! Sits between Area 1 (toward r1) and Area 0 (backbone, toward r3)
!
! Topology: [r1] --Area1-- [r2(ABR)] --Area0-- [r3] --Area2-- [r4]
!
! r2 generates Type-3 Summary LSAs — it takes LSAs from Area 1
! and advertises them into Area 0 (and vice versa).
!
! KEY TASK: After basic OSPF is working, configure inter-area
! summarization to collapse all 10.1.x.x/32 routes from Area 1
! into a single 10.1.0.0/22 summary in Area 0.
! ============================================================

interface Loopback0
 ! r2 loopback is in area 0 (backbone)
 ip address 10.0.0.2/32

! Link to r1 — segment 10.1.12.0/30 — in OSPF area 1
! r1 is .1, r2 is .2
interface Ethernet1
 no switchport
 description Link to r1 (10.1.12.0/30, r1=.1 r2=.2) — OSPF area 1
 ip address 10.1.12.2/30

! Link to r3 — segment 10.1.23.0/30 — in OSPF area 0
! r2 is .1, r3 is .2
interface Ethernet2
 no switchport
 description Link to r3 (10.1.23.0/30, r2=.1 r3=.2) — OSPF area 0
 ip address 10.1.23.1/30

! ============================================================
! TODO: Configure OSPF
!
! Step 1 — Basic OSPF with correct area assignments:
!   router ospf 1
!    ospf router-id 10.0.0.2
!    network 10.0.0.2/32 area 0
!    network 10.1.12.0/30 area 1     ! Ethernet1 is in area 1
!    network 10.1.23.0/30 area 0     ! Ethernet2 is in area 0
!    passive-interface Loopback0
!
! Step 2 — Inter-area summarization (this is what makes r2 interesting):
!   r2 sees individual /32 routes from r1 in area 1.
!   Without summarization, r2 floods each one as a separate Type-3 LSA
!   into area 0. With summarization, it sends ONE Type-3 LSA instead.
!
!   Add this inside the router ospf 1 block:
!     area 1 range 10.1.0.0/22
!
!   This tells r2: "For routes in area 1 that fall within 10.1.0.0/22,
!   advertise a single summary Type-3 LSA instead of individual LSAs."
!
! Step 3 — Verify with:
!   show ip ospf database         ! before and after — compare LSA counts
!   show ip ospf database summary ! Type-3 LSAs specifically
!
! Step 4 — Suppress a range entirely (not-advertise):
!   area 1 range 10.1.0.0/22 not-advertise
!   With this, the 10.1.0.0/22 prefix is SUPPRESSED — r3 and r4 have
!   NO route to area 1 prefixes. Useful for traffic engineering or
!   hiding an internal range from other areas.
!   Remove not-advertise to restore normal summarization.
! ============================================================
